WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.040
"What Is the Best Statin Cholesterol-Lowering Drug?"

00:00:07.040 --> 00:00:10.480
No fewer than 13 Nobel Prizes
have been awarded

00:00:10.480 --> 00:00:12.960
to scientists
researching cholesterol,

00:00:12.960 --> 00:00:14.400
culminating in a constellation

00:00:14.400 --> 00:00:17.760
of new cholesterol-lowering
modalities.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.120
Let me simplify.

00:00:19.120 --> 00:00:21.360
Here’s your liver,
a blood vessel,

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:23.120
and bit of your intestine.

00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:25.840
Our liver can make cholesterol
from scratch

00:00:25.840 --> 00:00:28.000
through a long series
of reactions,

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:30.080
I’ve shown just two here,

00:00:30.080 --> 00:00:31.600
which can then be packaged

00:00:31.600 --> 00:00:34.240
and end up as LDL
in our bloodstream

00:00:34.240 --> 00:00:37.280
to deliver cholesterol
throughout the body.

00:00:37.280 --> 00:00:39.280
Now, we don’t want
to have too much,

00:00:39.280 --> 00:00:41.600
as it can lodge
in our artery walls,

00:00:41.600 --> 00:00:43.760
become oxidized,
and trigger inflammation

00:00:43.760 --> 00:00:46.240
that can lead
to atherosclerotic plaque

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:47.680
and kill us.

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:52.320
So, our liver
also has LDL receptors

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.720
that pull LDL out of the blood

00:00:54.720 --> 00:00:57.120
and dispose of the cholesterol
through our bile

00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.680
by dumping it
into our digestive tract,

00:00:59.680 --> 00:01:02.800
presuming our intestines will
be packed with dietary fiber

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:06.640
that will trap it
and ultimately flush it away.

00:01:06.640 --> 00:01:09.840
But if the cholesterol
doesn’t get trapped,

00:01:09.840 --> 00:01:12.560
it can be reabsorbed
and get repackaged as LDL

00:01:12.560 --> 00:01:14.880
and re-enter our bloodstream.

00:01:14.880 --> 00:01:17.840
The same if we eat
a bunch of cholesterol.

00:01:17.840 --> 00:01:19.760
But the most important
dietary determinant

00:01:19.760 --> 00:01:22.160
of cholesterol
is saturated fat.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.600
That reduces the number
of LDL receptors;

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:28.720
so, more LDL stays in our blood.

00:01:28.720 --> 00:01:30.400
Okay, stick with me.

00:01:30.400 --> 00:01:34.560
There’s also a protein
called PCSK9 made in our liver

00:01:34.560 --> 00:01:38.000
that helps break down
LDL receptors.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.800
I did a video
about longevity syndromes,

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.840
where people who have
a faulty PCSK9 gene

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:47.040
luckily leading
to more LDL receptors

00:01:47.040 --> 00:01:50.240
and a 90% drop
in heart disease risk.

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:53.120
We can also boost
our number of LDL receptors

00:01:53.120 --> 00:01:55.760
to pull more LDL
out of our blood

00:01:55.760 --> 00:01:58.480
by cutting down
on saturated fat intake,

00:01:58.480 --> 00:02:01.120
but where do drugs come in?

00:02:01.120 --> 00:02:03.600
Well, we can cut down
on cholesterol production

00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.400
by blocking formation
here or here.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.320
That’s where statins come in

00:02:10.320 --> 00:02:13.680
or a new kid on the block
called bempedoic acid.

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:16.560
We could also block
the absorption and re-absorption

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:19.920
of cholesterol
with a drug called ezetimibe.

00:02:19.920 --> 00:02:22.800
Or, we can block
the production of PCSK9,

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:25.280
or the activity of PCSK9,

00:02:25.280 --> 00:02:27.680
so that we can maintain
our LDL receptors

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:31.520
to pull more LDL
out of our blood.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:37.280
These PCSK9 inhibitors
cost about $6,000 a year.

00:02:37.280 --> 00:02:41.760
Inclisiran is more like $10,000
for the first year.

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:44.400
And a combo of bempedoic acid
and ezetimibe

00:02:44.400 --> 00:02:46.960
is about $5,000 a year,

00:02:46.960 --> 00:02:49.760
whereas statins are typically
free with insurance,

00:02:49.760 --> 00:02:54.240
and even out of pocket could
just be like 50 bucks a year,

00:02:54.240 --> 00:02:56.960
especially since you can take
the two long-acting ones,

00:02:56.960 --> 00:02:58.800
atorvastatin and rosuvastatin,

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:02.000
every other day
and cut the cost in half.

00:03:02.000 --> 00:03:04.960
You can see why statins
are the first-line treatment

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:07.920
and have been
for nearly 40 years.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:10.400
We actually just lost Dr. Endo,

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.600
who discovered
the first statin.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:17.520
Microbes are constantly at war
with each other.

00:03:17.520 --> 00:03:19.680
We look to fungus
like penicillin

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.840
for compounds
that kill bacteria,

00:03:21.840 --> 00:03:25.680
and we look to bacteria
to find antifungal drugs.

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:29.120
Since some microbes rely
on cholesterol-like sterols,

00:03:29.120 --> 00:03:31.280
he was hoping to find
some other microbe

00:03:31.280 --> 00:03:34.640
that produced an anti-sterol
production compound,

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:36.240
and after years looking

00:03:36.240 --> 00:03:39.600
at approximately 6,000
different microbes,

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:42.800
in 1972,
he came across this one,

00:03:42.800 --> 00:03:46.160
a blue-green mold that produced
something that worked.

00:03:46.160 --> 00:03:48.960
If he had given up
after 5,000 attempts,

00:03:48.960 --> 00:03:51.680
then millions more people
may have died prematurely

00:03:51.680 --> 00:03:54.400
in the ensuing decades.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.280
If you are going to start
a statin, which one’s best?

00:03:57.280 --> 00:03:58.960
There are seven on the market,

00:03:58.960 --> 00:04:01.680
lowering LDL levels
from about 20% to 60%

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:03.600
based on brand and dose.

00:04:03.600 --> 00:04:06.400
In terms of comparative
effectiveness and safety

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:07.920
for primary prevention,

00:04:07.920 --> 00:04:11.280
atorvastatin and rosuvastatin
were most effective

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:13.920
in reducing
cardiovascular disease events,

00:04:13.920 --> 00:04:17.600
while atorvastatin appeared
to have the best safety profile.

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:20.800
No wonder atorvastatin
became the best-selling drug

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:22.400
of all time,

00:04:22.400 --> 00:04:25.360
raking in more
than a hundred billion dollars

00:04:25.360 --> 00:04:29.953
as Lipitor before it went off
patent in 2011.

